{
  "slug": "making-a-large-island",
  "title": "Making A Large Island",
  "topic": "graphs",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "pattern": "flood fill with component labelling and sizes",
  "statement": "You are given a rectangular grid of `0`s and `1`s. A cell holding `1` is land and a cell holding `0` is water. Land cells form an island when they are connected through shared edges (up, down, left, right); a single land cell is an island of size 1, and diagonal contact does not connect cells. The size of an island is the number of land cells it contains.\n\nYou may change at most one water cell into land, and you may also change nothing at all. After that single change, return the size of the largest island in the grid.\n\nWhen the grid holds no water, the answer is just the size of the one island that fills it. When the grid is entirely water, changing one cell is the best you can do and the answer is 1.\n\nFor example, in `[[1, 0], [0, 1]]` the two land cells are separate islands of size 1, and flipping either water cell joins both, so the answer is 3. In `[[1, 1], [1, 0]]` the answer is 4, because the grid already has an island of size 3 and flipping the last water cell joins it.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= grid.length <= 30` and `1 <= grid[i].length <= 30` and `0 <= grid[i][j] <= 1`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "grid = [[0]]",
      "output": "1",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "grid = [[1]]",
      "output": "1",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(grid: number[][])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Flipping a water cell merges it with every island it touches. Work out which islands exist and how big each one is before flipping anything.",
    "Flood fill every land cell to give it a component id and store each component's size. A flipped cell is then worth 1 plus the sizes of the distinct ids among its four neighbours.",
    "Deduplicate the neighbour ids, because one component can touch the flipped cell on two sides. Also remember that the answer may come from an existing island when no flip improves anything."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force tries every water cell: flip it to land, run a flood fill over the whole grid to measure the largest island, then flip it back, which costs O(m * n) per water cell and O((m * n)^2) overall. The optimal version pays for one flood fill only. It walks the grid once, assigns every land cell a component id, and records the size of each component; afterwards a water cell is evaluated in constant time by collecting the distinct ids of its four neighbours and adding their sizes to the 1 the flipped cell itself contributes. The answer is the best of that value and the largest existing component, which covers grids with no water at all.",
    "why_optimal": "Every cell has to be read at least once and each cell has four neighbours, so O(m * n) time is the floor, and the id table plus the size array cost O(m * n) space. The brute force is a factor of m * n slower because it re-derives connectivity that the first pass already produced. With at most 900 cells both versions sit well inside the time limit.",
    "pitfalls": "Counting one component twice when the flipped cell touches it from two sides, which is why the neighbour ids must be collected into a set before summing. Handling a grid with no water: the answer is the largest existing island, so the running maximum over component sizes has to seed the answer before any flip is considered. An all-water grid must return 1, not 0, and a grid holding a single land cell must return 1 as well. Recursive flood fill can nest as deep as the number of land cells, so an explicit stack or a raised recursion limit is required."
  },
